077,594,19131,264,48968,311,39721,279,23606,13,6771,510,59,9640,88,284,1124,839,62,17,856,624,59,2533,16429,419,48968,11,582,646,18130,279,23606,438,510,59,9640,59,839,62,17,19788,839,62,23,856,8,284,1124,839,62,23,379,624,59,2533,5847,11,582,1184,311,3158,1124,11520,839,62,23,856,57758,304,3793,315,2331,220,17,89936,1011,13,79540,429,510,59,9640,59,839,62,23,856,284,1124,37018,35702,839,62,17,856,15170,59,839,62,17,220,23,27275,59,2533,12549,1124,11520,839,62,17,220,23,284,220,18,59,701,582,614,510,59,9640,59,839,62,23,856,284,1124,37018,35702,839,62,17,856,15170,18,92,284,1124,37018,90,88,15170,18,27275,59,2533,3136,3696,10607,1124,11520,839,62,23,856,284,1124,37018,90,88,15170,18,11035,8,1182,1119,279,23606,11,582,633,510,59,9640,59,839,62,17,1124,2359,7,1124,37018,90,88,15170,18,92,1124,1291,8,284,1124,839,62,23,379,624,59,2533,7039,11,582,1184,311,3158,1124,11520,839,62,23,379,57758,304,3793,315,2331,220,17,89936,1011,13,12091,279,2297,315,2331,14806,11,582,614,510,59,9640,59,839,62,23,379,284,1124,37018,35702,839,62,17,379,15170,59,839,62,17,220,23,92,284,1124,37018,35702,839,62,17,379,15170,18,27275,59,2533,3136,3696,10607,1124,11520,839,62,23,379,284,1124,37018,35702,839,62,17,379,15170,18,11035,8,1119,279,23606,11,582,633,510,59,9640,59,839,62,17,1124,2359,7,1124,37018,90,88,15170,18,92,1124,1291,8,284,1124,37018,35702,839,62,17,379,15170,18,27275,59,21675,522,37420,91339,29,151645,198,151644,872,198,2610,1231,312,18883,55856,279,3491,323,18130,279,6291,504,279,7167,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nFind $(\\log_2 x)^2$ if $\\log_2 (\\log_8 x) = \\log_8 (\\log_2 x).$\n\nPartial draft:\n<partial_draft>\nTo solve the problem, we start with the given equation:\n\\[\n\\log_2 (\\log_8 x) = \\log_8 (\\log_2 x).\n\\]\n\nFirst, let's introduce a substitution to simplify the equation. Let:\n\\[\ny = \\log_2 x.\n\\]\n\nUsing this substitution, we can rewrite the equation as:\n\\[\n\\log_2 (\\log_8 x) = \\log_8 y.\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to express \\(\\log_8 x\\) in terms of base 2 logarithms. Recall that:\n\\[\n\\log_8 x = \\frac{\\log_2 x}{\\log_2 8}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\log_2 8 = 3\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\log_8 x = \\frac{\\log_2 x}{3} = \\frac{y}{3}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(\\log_8 x = \\frac{y}{3}\\) back into the equation, we get:\n\\[\n\\log_2 \\left( \\frac{y}{3} \\right) = \\log_8 y.\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to express \\(\\log_8 y\\) in terms of base 2 logarithms. Using the change of base formula, we have:\n\\[\n\\log_8 y = \\frac{\\log_2 y}{\\log_2 8} = \\frac{\\log_2 y}{3}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(\\log_8 y = \\frac{\\log_2 y}{3}\\) into the equation, we get:\n\\[\n\\log_2 \\left( \\frac{y}{3} \\right) = \\frac{\\log_2 y}{3}.\n\\]\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"You may re-analyze the problem and rewrite the solution from the beginning.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"f3f42968b6ac957b21bb79b065bccf523f59479ef4dffa47fe2c742358f414aa"},"seconds":55.65888953208923,"seed":168571000379642811,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem step by step, we start with the given equation:\n\\[\n\\log_2 (\\log_8 x) = \\log_8 (\\log_2 x).\n\\]\n\nFirst, let's introduce a substitution to simplify the equation. Let:\n\\[\ny = \\log_2 x.\n\\]\n\nUsing this substitution, we can rewrite the equation as:\n\\[\n\\log_2 (\\log_8 x) = \\log_8 y.\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to express \\(\\log_8 x\\) in terms of base 2 logarithms. Recall the change of base formula:\n\\[\n\\log_8 x = \\frac{\\log_2 x}{\\log_2 8}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\log_2 8 = 3\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\log_8 x = \\frac{\\log_2 x}{3} = \\frac{y}{3}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(\\log_8 x = \\frac{y}{3}\\) back into the equation, we get:\n\\[\n\\log_2 \\left( \\frac{y}{3} \\right) = \\log_8 y.\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to express \\(\\log_8 y\\) in terms of base 2 logarithms. Using the change of base formula, we have:\n\\[\n\\log_8 y = \\frac{\\log_2 y}{\\log_2 8} = \\frac{\\log_2 y}{3}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(\\log_8 y = \\frac{\\log_2 y}{3}\\) into the equation, we get:\n\\[\n\\log_2 \\left( \\frac{y}{3} \\right) = \\frac{\\log_2 y}{3}.\n\\]\n\nUsing the logarithm property \\(\\log_b \\left( \\frac{a}{c} \\right) = \\log_b a - \\log_b c\\), we can rewrite the left side as:\n\\[\n\\log_2 \\left( \\frac{y}{3} \\right) = \\log_2 y - \\log_2 3.\n\\]\n\nSo the equation becomes:\n\\[\n\\log_2 y - \\log_2 3 = \\frac{\\log_2 y}{3}.\n\\]\n\nLet \\(z = \\log_2 y\\). Then the equation becomes:\n\\[\nz - \\log_2 3 = \\frac{z}{3}.\n\\]\n\nTo solve for \\(z\\), we first eliminate the fraction by multiplying through by 3:\n\\[\n3z - 3 \\log_2 3 = z.\n\\]\n\nRearranging terms, we get:\n\\[\n2z = 3 \\log_2 3.\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(z\\), we have:\n\\[\nz = \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(z = \\log_2 y\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\log_2 y = \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}.\n\\]\n\nRecall that \\(y = \\log_2 x\\), so:\n\\[\n\\log_2 (\\log_2 x) = \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}.\n\\]\n\nWe need to find \\((\\log_2 x)^2\\). Let \\(a = \\log_2 x\\), then:\n\\[\n\\log_2 a = \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}.\n\\]\n\nRewriting in exponential form, we have:\n\\[\na = 2^{\\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}} = (2^{\\log_2 3})^{\\frac{3}{2}} = 3^{\\frac{3}{2}} = \\sqrt{27}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, \\((\\log_2 x)^2 = a^2 = (\\sqrt{27})^2 = 27\\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{27}.\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{27}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To solve the problem step by step, we start with the given equation:\n\\[\n\\log_2 (\\log_8 x) = \\log_8 (\\log_2 x).\n\\]\n\nFirst, let's introduce a substitution to simplify the equation. Let:\n\\[\ny = \\log_2 x.\n\\]\n\nUsing this substitution, we can rewrite the equation as:\n\\[\n\\log_2 (\\log_8 x) = \\log_8 y.\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to express \\(\\log_8 x\\) in terms of base 2 logarithms. Recall the change of base formula:\n\\[\n\\log_8 x = \\frac{\\log_2 x}{\\log_2 8}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\log_2 8 = 3\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\log_8 x = \\frac{\\log_2 x}{3} = \\frac{y}{3}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(\\log_8 x = \\frac{y}{3}\\) back into the equation, we get:\n\\[\n\\log_2 \\left( \\frac{y}{3} \\right) = \\log_8 y.\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to express \\(\\log_8 y\\) in terms of base 2 logarithms. Using the change of base formula, we have:\n\\[\n\\log_8 y = \\frac{\\log_2 y}{\\log_2 8} = \\frac{\\log_2 y}{3}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(\\log_8 y = \\frac{\\log_2 y}{3}\\) into the equation, we get:\n\\[\n\\log_2 \\left( \\frac{y}{3} \\right) = \\frac{\\log_2 y}{3}.\n\\]\n\nUsing the logarithm property \\(\\log_b \\left( \\frac{a}{c} \\right) = \\log_b a - \\log_b c\\), we can rewrite the left side as:\n\\[\n\\log_2 \\left( \\frac{y}{3} \\right) = \\log_2 y - \\log_2 3.\n\\]\n\nSo the equation becomes:\n\\[\n\\log_2 y - \\log_2 3 = \\frac{\\log_2 y}{3}.\n\\]\n\nLet \\(z = \\log_2 y\\). Then the equation becomes:\n\\[\nz - \\log_2 3 = \\frac{z}{3}.\n\\]\n\nTo solve for \\(z\\), we first eliminate the fraction by multiplying through by 3:\n\\[\n3z - 3 \\log_2 3 = z.\n\\]\n\nRearranging terms, we get:\n\\[\n2z = 3 \\log_2 3.\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(z\\), we have:\n\\[\nz = \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(z = \\log_2 y\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\log_2 y = \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}.\n\\]\n\nRecall that \\(y = \\log_2 x\\), so:\n\\[\n\\log_2 (\\log_2 x) = \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}.\n\\]\n\nWe need to find \\((\\log_2 x)^2\\). Let \\(a = \\log_2 x\\), then:\n\\[\n\\log_2 a = \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}.\n\\]\n\nRewriting in exponential form, we have:\n\\[\na = 2^{\\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}} = (2^{\\log_2 3})^{\\frac{3}{2}} = 3^{\\frac{3}{2}} = \\sqrt{27}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, \\((\\log_2 x)^2 = a^2 = (\\sqrt{27})^2 = 27\\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{27}.\n\\]","id":"intermediate_algebra:434"}
